   "id": "aae77681",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "data = pd.read_csv(\"enron_spam_data.csv\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5c37eaae",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Initial Thoughts on Text Cleaning\n",
    "\n",
    "In my previous version, I converted all letters to lowercase. At that time, I thought it would make the comparison fairer. But after looking at the data more carefully, I realized that I might also be losing some useful information.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, words like `money` or `off` can appear in any type of email. However, when they are written in uppercase, they might have a different meaning or indicate emphasis and advertising. So, I think it's better to keep this information for now and investigate its importance later.\n",
    "\n",
    "I'm still not sure about URLs. Having a link in an email is not unusual, and normal emails can contain them too. I'll look into whether this feature actually helps with spam detection later.\n",
    "\n",
    "Characters like `\\n` and similar formatting details don't seem very useful to me at this stage. I'd rather avoid making the text more complicated with information that might not help the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "Punctuation marks seem more interesting because they might reveal different patterns in spam and normal emails.\n",
    "\n",
    "Numbers can also contain useful information, so I don't want to remove them for now. I'll leave HTML for a later investigation since I haven't formed a clear opinion about it yet.\n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, I don't think email addresses provide much useful information for the model, but I want to test this assumption before removing them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "203168d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
