  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9ac60530",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now I want to reduce the model's vocabulary size. Many words need to be converted to their base forms. The model isn't supposed to be a spelling checker. It just needs to understand the meaning of the words, not their exact forms."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 45,
   "id": "86bad1d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def get_wordnet_pos(tag):\n",
    "    if tag.startswith('J'):\n",
    "        return wordnet.ADJ\n",
    "    elif tag.startswith('V'):\n",
    "        return wordnet.VERB\n",
    "    elif tag.startswith('N'):\n",
    "        return wordnet.NOUN\n",
    "    elif tag.startswith('R'):\n",
    "        return wordnet.ADV\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        return wordnet.NOUN\n",
    "\n",
    "lemmatizer = WordNetLemmatizer()\n",
    "\n",
    "def lemmatize_text(text):\n",
    "    words = word_tokenize(text)\n",
    "    tagged = pos_tag(words)\n",
    "\n",
    "    lemmas = [\n",
    "        lemmatizer.lemmatize(word, get_wordnet_pos(tag))\n",
    "        for word, tag in tagged\n",
    "    ]\n",
    "\n",
    "    return \" \".join(lemmas)\n",
    "\n",
    "X_train[\"Message\"] = X_train[\"Message\"].apply(lemmatize_text)\n",
    "X_test[\"Message\"] = X_test[\"Message\"].apply(lemmatize_text)\n",
    "X_val[\"Message\"] = X_val[\"Message\"].apply(lemmatize_text)\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6421844e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### TF-IDF\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we're getting to the main idea of this project: using the TF-IDF algorithm.\n",
    "\n",
    "The more I read about the math and ideas behind these algorithms, the more I realize how cool and interesting they are.\n",
    "\n",
    "The idea is to give each word a certain weight:\n",
    "- A word that appears many times in one email gets more importance.\n",
    "- But a word that appears in almost every email gets less importance.\n",
    "\n",
    "It also removes common words like `is`, `a`, and `the`.\n",
    "\n",
    "I've also set it up to extract not only single words, but also pairs of consecutive words (bigrams), so it can keep some of the meaning that comes from words appearing together.\n",
    "\n",
    "The `Subject` column might contain useful information for email classification. But for now, I only want to focus on the email body, so I'm leaving it out.\n",
    "One detail: `TfidfVectorizer` uses `lowercase=True` by default, and its default token pattern does not keep punctuation as standalone features. So although capitalization and punctuation remain in the raw `Message` column, the current TF-IDF representation does not preserve those details as separate features.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "bbeaf5f5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.svm import LinearSVC\n",
    "\n",
    "extra_X = [\"url_count\", \"emoji\", \"word_count\", \"log_char_count\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "preprocessor = ColumnTransformer(\n",
    "    transformers=[\n",
    "        (\n",
    "            \"text\",\n",
    "            TfidfVectorizer(\n",
    "                stop_words=\"english\",\n",
    "                ngram_range=(1, 2),\n",
    "                min_df=5,\n",
    "                max_df=0.95\n",
    "            ),\n",
    "            \"Message\"\n",
    "        ),\n",
    "        (\n",
    "            \"numeric\",\n",
    "            MinMaxScaler(),\n",
    "            extra_X\n",
    "        )\n",
    "    ]\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "main_pipeline = Pipeline([\n",
    "    (\"preprocessor\", preprocessor),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"model\",\n",
    "        LinearSVC(\n",
    "            class_weight=\"balanced\",\n",
    "            random_state=42\n",
    "        )\n",
    "    )\n",
    "])\n"
   ]
  },
