   "source": [
    "Honestly, after reading these emails, I had a simple thought.\n",
    "\n",
    "LinearSVC can't understand meaning the way humans do. It makes its decisions based on the TF-IDF feature weights and the four numerical features (`url_count`, `emoji`, `word_count`, and `log_char_count`).\n",
    "\n",
    "In most of these emails, I can see patterns that match the labels predicted by the model. But sometimes, when I actually read an email carefully, I can tell that the model might have made a mistake.\n",
    "\n",
    "So I came up with an idea. After running enough experiments and choosing the best-performing model, I'll test it on a completely different dataset, outside of Enron, to see how well it performs on data it hasn't seen before.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c083355a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "-----"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e119e390",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finding the Best Threshold\n",
    "\n",
    "Now I'll find the threshold that gives the highest F1-score on the validation set. This lets me choose a balance between precision and recall using an explicit metric.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 57,
   "id": "702242c4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Best threshold: 0.057301239376715773\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "scores = model3.decision_function(X_val)\n",
    "\n",
    "precision, recall, thresholds = precision_recall_curve(\n",
    "    y_val,\n",
    "    scores,\n",
    "    pos_label=\"spam\"\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "f1_scores = (\n",
    "    2 * precision[:-1] * recall[:-1] /\n",
    "    (precision[:-1] + recall[:-1] + 1e-12)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "best_t = thresholds[np.argmax(f1_scores)]\n",
    "print(\"Best threshold:\", best_t)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
